## Current Synthesis

### Idea

Make installation and every upgrade path converge the workspace toward the complete feature set of
the selected Tool Shed release while preserving one internally consistent authority model at every
intermediate state. Treat a partial migration as an explicit, diagnosable operating mode rather
than a nominally successful installation whose commands silently choose different sources of truth.

The immediate trigger is [GitHub issue #66](https://github.com/PC-Redemption/tool_shed/issues/66):
a released `0.52.1` client could contain a schema-2 database in `shadow` mode with no imported
documents while file-backed `work/` remained authoritative. File campaign and roadmap commands
continued to work, but readiness, planning order, Doctor, and dashboard paths treated database
presence or schema level as authority and saw an empty project.

A subsequent field convergence at `workpc:E:\dev\high_point\7520708_DispTest_FW` confirmed the
failure shape and exposed additional boundaries. The workspace ultimately reached a clean,
checkpoint-current schema-6 Hybrid state, but only after a multi-step manual sequence. It spent
roughly four and a half hours at schema 2 in `shadow` mode before four documents were imported,
authority was cut over, schemas 3 through 6 were applied, outcome/readiness state was populated,
views were rendered, and the result was committed. That is a successful recovery, not evidence that
the released installer supplied convergence.

### Why It Matters

Shipping code for a feature is not useful when the installed workspace cannot safely reach the
state that enables it. Worse, split authority produces credible but contradictory answers: a valid
Idea Brief can disappear from one command, artifact counts can become zero in another, and newer
outcome capabilities can remain unavailable without the install summary saying so. This weakens
operator trust and makes an upgrade look complete when it has only updated executable files.

### Desired Outcome

- Installation and upgrade evaluate the current workspace against the selected release's complete
  migration and feature requirements, apply every safe automatic convergence step, and report the
  applied, deferred, and blocked results.
- A single authoritative state contract selects file, shadow, or Hybrid behavior for every
  consumer. Database presence and schema number alone never imply document authority.
- Until guarded cutover succeeds, readiness, planning order, Doctor, dashboard, PRM, campaign, and
  index behavior all use a working file-authoritative fallback and agree on identities and counts.
- When convergence needs owner authority, the operation emits one bounded decision that states the
  impact, unavailable features, rollback path, and exact continuation command.
- Approved conversion preserves documents, stable identities, relationships, lifecycle, planning
  order, campaign state, and outcome state while migrating through every supported schema step.
- Post-install verification proves that all relevant commands agree on authority, inventory, and
  feature availability; no path reports unconditional success without that concise summary.
- Runtime prerequisites are verified by capability, not merely by Python version. A read-capable
  interpreter that cannot execute Tool Shed's guarded SQLite writes is reported as degraded or
  blocked and cannot receive a `HEALTHY` mutation-capable verdict.
- Conversion proves semantic relationship parity as well as body-byte parity. Resolvable legacy
  parent/project-map/output links become immutable-ID relationships; ambiguous links remain
  explicit findings rather than silently surviving only as path text.
- Convergence records failed as well as successful steps with bounded diagnostic codes and exact
  recovery actions, so a later review does not depend on console or chat history.
- Structural/schema convergence, semantic historical backfill, and readiness judgment are separate
  action classes. Install verification does not create semantic planning state merely to prove a
  feature can run.

## Constraints And Non-Goals

- Existing guarded archive, project identity, parity, idempotence, checkpoint/rebuild, rollback,
  stale-state, and authority-cutover requirements remain intact.
- Dirty state, interrupted conversion, unsupported environments, credentials, and protected
  boundaries must fail safely without converting a split state into accepted authority.
- New workspaces, file-only workspaces, empty and populated schema-2 shadows, older authoritative
  Hybrid schemas, interrupted conversions, and rollback paths all need deterministic coverage.
- Compatibility must be release-aware: a workspace may legitimately remain file-authoritative,
  but every shipped command must honor that same state and report its feature limits.
- The solution should reuse migration and audit mechanisms already present instead of creating a
  second installer, tracker, or authority database.
- Interpreter, SQLite feature support, scheduled-task executable identity, and other runtime-bound
  consumers are part of the feature-availability contract; a workspace-local instruction pin is a
  mitigation, not proof of portable convergence.
- Explicit non-goal: perform an unsafe implicit authority switch merely to make the installation
  appear fully converged.
- Explicit non-goal: implement separate ad-hoc authority checks in each command.
- Explicit non-goal: claim feature availability because code exists when its required state or
  migration has not been established.

## Possibilities And Tradeoffs

| Possibility | Benefits | Costs / Risks | Evidence / Status |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| One shared authority resolver plus file-compatible consumers | Removes divergent heuristics and restores current file/shadow workspaces quickly | Every consumer must route through the contract; compatibility logic may live longer than desired | Required foundation; issue evidence identifies multiple commands that currently infer authority differently |
| Declarative release convergence registry and ordered migration planner | Makes the selected release's feature prerequisites, safe automatic steps, blockers, and next actions explicit | Requires a versioned dependency model and careful idempotence across install/update entry points | Strong candidate for preventing future shipped-but-unreachable features |
| One transactional convergence orchestrator used by install and all upgrade paths | Produces a uniform summary and bounded decision surface; centralizes resume and post-check behavior | Can become too broad if it absorbs domain-specific migration logic | Prefer a thin orchestrator over existing guarded domain migrations |
| Eagerly convert and cut over every workspace during update | Minimizes compatibility duration when successful | Violates explicit authority boundaries and increases data-loss/rollback risk | Rejected as a universal default |
| Report degradation without attempting safe migrations | Simple and transparent | Leaves avoidable feature gaps and does not meet the convergence requirement | Useful only as the fallback after safe automatic steps are exhausted |
| Patch only the commands named in #66 | Small immediate change | Future consumers can recreate split authority; does not address migration reachability or install truth | Insufficient except as an early compatibility slice |
| Capability-based runtime preflight using a disposable SQLite probe | Detects whether required JSON checks/triggers and hardened pragmas can execute before workspace mutation | Must remain side-effect-free and version-independent; needs Windows coverage | Required by the field failure: Python 3.11.2/SQLite 3.39.4 could read the final database but rejected guarded trigger writes |
| Durable convergence journal covering success, failure, deferment, and resume | Makes partial progress and exact recovery inspectable across sessions | Must not become a second authority or retain unsafe raw exception data | Preferred replacement for the opaque `TSU-801 / unknown` field report and scattered migration files |
| Relationship-parity extraction during document conversion | Converts resolvable legacy path links to immutable-ID edges and makes context retrieval complete | Header/link vocabularies need explicit mappings; ambiguous links require review | Required by the field fixture where `INV-0001` retained a `Parent:` header but the relationship table and `related/context` results were empty |
| Disposable or read-only conformance checks for semantic features | Proves readiness/outcome commands are callable without changing live planning meaning | Some paths may require purpose-built validation modes or fixtures | Preferred over creating readiness reviews or historical outcome state as an installation smoke test |

## Open Questions

- What is the canonical authority-resolution API and minimal state machine for absent database,
  file authority, shadow import, qualified shadow, Hybrid authority, and recovery states?
- Should feature prerequisites be declared in one release manifest, derived from migration/schema
  registries, or assembled from domain-owned capability checks behind a common interface?
- Which current convergence steps are provably safe and automatic, and exactly where is explicit
  owner authority still required (conversion, cutover, retirement, or all three)?
- How should every install/update entry point persist and resume a partially completed convergence
  plan without letting the plan itself become a second authority surface?
- What is the smallest file-authoritative compatibility adapter for readiness and planning order
  that preserves stable identity and avoids duplicating future database semantics?
- Should dashboard and Doctor consume the same normalized authority/inventory capsule as CLI
  commands, or call the resolver independently with a shared conformance suite?
- What exact feature-availability vocabulary distinguishes available, compatible fallback,
  degraded, awaiting decision, blocked, and unsupported without overwhelming routine installs?
- How are sequential schema upgrades ordered when document, campaign, closure-lineage,
  loop-finding, and persistent-execution schemas have different prerequisites or rollback scopes?
- What constitutes post-install agreement: identical authority ID and artifact counts only, or also
  identity/relationship/lifecycle digests and outcome-state invariants?
- How should an interrupted operation prove whether to resume, roll back, or retain the previous
  authority without trusting partially written progress markers?
- Which concrete SQLite capabilities must be probed for every shipped schema and mutation family,
  and can one disposable probe faithfully cover document, closure, loop-finding, and campaign
  execution triggers on every supported runtime?
- Should Doctor expose separate read-health and mutation-readiness verdicts, or should a failed
  disposable mutation-capability probe make the overall result `DEGRADED` with an exact runtime
  remediation?
- What bounded diagnostic fields would have made the field `TSU-801 / unknown` preflight failure
  actionable without storing paths, exception text, prompts, or secrets?
- Which legacy Markdown relationships are unambiguous enough to migrate automatically, and how
  should unresolved parent/output references affect cutover and Doctor?
- Which semantic backfills are required for compatibility versus optional enrichment, and what
  explicit owner decision is needed before each class is applied?
- How should the installer verify and, when authorized, converge scheduler/service interpreters so
  background consumers cannot use a different SQLite capability set from interactive commands?

## Decisions

- Authority selection is a shared product contract, not a command-local heuristic.
- `storage_mode` participates in authority selection; schema support or database existence alone is
  never sufficient.
- Safe automatic convergence is the default, but conversion and cutover remain guarded when their
  established authority requirements demand it.
- A partial result must preserve a functional single-authority fallback and truthfully enumerate
  every unavailable or degraded feature with an exact next action.
- Install and update are responsible for state convergence and feature truth, not only file
  replacement.
- Domain migrations may remain independently testable; orchestration should compose them rather
  than replace their guards and rollback contracts.
- Feature availability includes mutation capability under every configured interactive and
  background runtime; successful read-only audit is not sufficient.
- A convergence ledger must retain bounded failure classification and resume state in addition to
  the existing successful migration ledger.
- Post-install checks must not manufacture readiness reviews, verdicts, relationships, or
  historical outcome truth solely as probes.
- Relationship parity includes durable immutable-ID edges, not only preserved Markdown bytes.

